"Ostmann's inverse Goldbach problem: unsolved problem in mathematics — Papex (https://papex.org/problems/ostmann-inverse-goldbach-conjecture)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # Ostmann's inverse Goldbach problem ...     This repository contains mathematical manuscripts and supporting proof artifacts produced by an unnamed internal OpenAI model.\n\nOpen problem · Mathematics · Open\n\n# Ostmann's inverse Goldbach problem\n\nCan a set differing from the primes in finitely many elements be a sumset A + B of sets with at least two elements?\n\n## Summary\n\nOstmann conjectured that the primes are additively indecomposable in this sense.\n\n## AI-assisted claims\n\nProof claimed · 06 Oct 2026\n\n### The additive indecomposability of the primes\n\n> “We prove Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture: no set differing from the primes by finitely many elements can be written as $A+B$, where A and B are sets of nonnegative integers with at least two elements each.”\n\nBy\n    OpenAI\nAI involvement\n    This repository contains mathematical manuscripts and supporting proof artifacts produced by an unnamed internal OpenAI model.\nModel\n    an unreleased internal OpenAI model, as stated\nWhere\n    openai/math repository\nFormal proof\n    Lean 4, published by the source\nChecked\n    04 Nov 2026\n\nClaims as their sources state them. Papex has not checked them; the status stays the source’s.\n\n## Source\n\nOpen, as listed by Christian Elsholtz (Mathematika 48, 2001) · The inverse Goldbach problem. Checked 04 Nov 2026.\n\nThe status is the source’s. Papex does not decide whether a problem is solved.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe additive indecomposability of the primes · oai-math-revised (https://mathcommunity.github.io/oai-math-revised/papers/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 days ago;   * OpenAI math release, Ostmann’s inverse Goldbach conjecture: Lean scope note (lean/docs/013.md). ...   * Ostmann's inverse Goldbach theorem and the infinite-summands case: statement, proof.\n\n# The additive indecomposability of the primes\n\nOpenAI · September 24, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture: no set differing from the primes by finitely many elements can be written as $A+B$, where A and B are sets of nonnegative integers with at least two elements each.\n\nPDF of the manuscript\n\nIf the PDF does not display here, open it directly: Open PDF.\n\n## Notes\n\nNo notes yet. Add a reference, a comment or your name with the buttons above, or edit NOTES.md on GitHub.\n\n## Automatically collected notes (unreviewed)\n\nwork in progress\n\nCollected automatically by AI tools (Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra, 2026-10-30) and not checked by a person. May be wrong or incomplete. Report an error.\n\n## Follow-up works\n\n  * Paolo Leonetti, On the inverse Goldbach problem (arXiv:2610.15758, 11 Oct 2026). Proves upper and lower counting-function bounds for a hypothetical decomposition of the primes and a lower bound for ternary sumsets; its introduction cites this paper and says the claim \"has not yet been verified by the math community.\"\n\n## News and blogs\n\n  * Thomas Bloom, Erdős problems in the OpenAI proofs (erdosproblems.com blog, 8 Oct 2026). Has a section headed \"Primes are not a sumset\" that summarizes the claimed result as Problem 431, notes that the proof has been formalised, and says the PDF is 80 pages. [family-level]\n\n## Lean formalization\n\nOpenAI provides Lean files for this family. The scope note, lean/docs/013.md, says which results they cover.\n\nEach result is stated in a file in `lean/ComparatorChallenges/`. These files end in `sorry` on purpose: they only state the result. The proof is in a separate module, and Lean's comparator is the tool for checking that the module proves exactly what the statement file says.\n\n  * Ostmann's inverse Goldbach theorem and the infinite-summands case: statement, proof. Comparator run of 2026-10-26: accepted (log).\n  * Additive indecomposability of the primes up to finite changes: statement, proof. Comparator run of 2026-10-26: accepted (log).\n\nThe comparator runs are described, with what they show and what they do not, in docs/LEAN_CHECKS.md.\n\nNobody has checked whether the Lean statements say what the paper claims.\n\n## Family 013: Ostmann’s inverse Goldbach conjecture\n\nProves that no finite modification of the primes can be written as $A+B$ with $A,B\\subseteq\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ each containing at least two elements. This resolves Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture on additive indecomposability.\n\nThis is the only paper in this family.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSplit the primes? Ostmann’s inverse Goldbach conjecture | AI Proved What? (https://aiprovedwhat.com/episodes/013-ostmanns-inverse-goldbach-conjecture/)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 5 days ago; Ostmann’s inverse Goldbach conjecture: the primes are additively indecomposable ... The proof behind it is about 4,400 files of OpenAI’s code, about 365,000 lines; a second, separate Lean proof of the same theorem by a different route adds about 3,200 files.\n\nEpisode 013, October 2026\n\n# Split the primes?\n\nOstmann’s inverse Goldbach conjecture: the primes are additively indecomposable\n\nCovers result 013 in OpenAI’s catalog: its one paper. See its connections to other results and the work it cites.\n\n4:23. English captions available. The video loads from YouTube when you press play.\n\n## Versions of this episode\n\n  1. Picture-first remake, current (October 24, 2026) · shown above\n  2. Original · Watch the previous version (Unlisted on YouTube)\n\n## Chapters\n\n  1. 0:00 Splitting a set\n  2. 0:44 A near miss\n  3. 1:36 The residue clock\n  4. 2:17 The claim\n  5. 2:34 How the proof goes\n  6. 3:27 Lean, and the caveats\n\n## The claim, in plain words\n\nFor sets A and B of nonnegative integers, A + B is the set of all sums a + b. Many sets split this way: every number from 10 on is {0, 1} plus the even numbers from 10 on. In his 1956 book Additive Zahlentheorie, Hans-Heinrich Ostmann conjectured that the primes do not: no two sets, each with at least two elements, have exactly the primes as their sums, even after adding or removing finitely many numbers. This is the inverse Goldbach problem, and Erdős problem #431.\n\nResult 013 claims to prove it: if |A|, |B| ≥ 2, then A + B and the primes differ in infinitely many numbers. Either infinitely many primes are not sums, or infinitely many composite numbers are.\n\n## What it doesn’t claim\n\nThe condition that both sets have at least two elements matters: {0} plus the primes is the primes. The result is qualitative; it gives no bound on how many exceptions there must be up to x.\n\n## Where it stands\n\nPosted as a preprint\n    OpenAI, dated September 24, 2026; released October 6, 2026\n\nUnder expert review\n    Not yet\n\nPeer reviewed\n    Not yet\n\nChecked in Lean\n    OpenAI published two separate Lean proofs of the main theorem (scope note); we have not run the check\n\nLast checked October 30, 2026. The claim has not been withdrawn, and we found no public critique. Erdős problem #431 is still listed as open on erdosproblems.com.\n\nA collision estimate shows that, on average over primes, the two parts are about equal in size.\n  3. No structure. The summands cannot correlate with quadratic characters, even with centres that move from prime to prime; higher-order characters follow by repeated Cauchy–Schwarz transfers.\n  4. Coverage. Every large prime must be a sum. A positive statistic built from this coverage is compared, through a finite-field comparison of binary trees, with what the decorrelation allows; the two cannot both hold. The proof does not use the Green–Harper conjecture.\n\n## The Lean statement\n    \n    `theorem main : ∀ A B : Set ℕ, A.Nontrivial → B.Nontrivial →\n        Set.Infinite ((A + B) ∆ {n : ℕ | Nat.Prime n})`\n\nThis is the statement in the challenge file: `Nontrivial` means at least two elements, and ∆ is the symmetric difference. The proof behind it is about 4,400 files of OpenAI’s code, about 365,000 lines; a second, separate Lean proof of the same theorem by a different route adds about 3,200 files. A passing Comparator check would guarantee the statement from Lean’s standard axioms. Neither proof is in OpenAI’s formalization catalog yet, and we have not run the check.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOstmann’s inverse Goldbach conjecture, family 013 | Plectis Atlas (https://wcook04.github.io/plectis-atlas/families/013.html)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; A passing Comparator run shows that OpenAI’s proof proves the exact Lean statement; it does not show that the statement means the same as the paper’s theorem. ... Ostmann’s inverse Goldbach theorem and the infinite-summands case\nThe statements each paper opens with are on its reading page.\n\n### The additive indecomposability of the primes\n\n24 September 2026, current edition.\n\nRead it herePDF\n\nWe prove Ostmann’s inverse Goldbach conjecture: no set differing from the primes by finitely many elements can be written as \\(A+B\\), where A and B are sets of nonnegative integers with at least two elements each.\n\n## What did OpenAI write in Lean?\n\nOpenAI selected two Lean statements for this family. Each Lean statement below stands beside the paper theorem it is a possible match for, with the groups’ Comparator runs above it.\n\nA passing Comparator run shows that OpenAI’s proof proves the exact Lean statement; it does not show that the statement means the same as the paper’s theorem.\n\n  1. Ostmann’s inverse Goldbach theorem and the infinite-summands case\n\n`OstmannComplete`, a supporting statement in OpenAI’s release\n\nPaper theorem\n    Possible match for Theorem 2.3 in The additive indecomposability of the primes, found from shared wording; not reviewed.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenai 2026 additive indecomposability primes — Erdős Problems (https://erdosproblems.ai/wiki/library/primes/openai_2026_additive_indecomposability_primes)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: yesterday; theorem_1_1: Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture as the manuscript claims it: no sumset of two sets of nonnegative integers with at least two elements each differs from the primes in finitely many elements; reduced by a sieve lemma to the two-infinite-summands Theorem 2.3. ... The manuscript's own README in the release carries only the title, the author line \"OpenAI\", the date and the citation block above; neither it nor the paper says anything further about how the text was produced or checked. ... The release's Lean catalog (`lean/formalization.yaml`) does not name this manuscript, but the release's family page `lean/docs/013.md` names it as the accompanying paper of a formalization whose scope it describes as Ostmann's conjecture for sets of nonnegative integers with at least two elements each, together with the two-infinite-summands case; the comparator statement files it names are `lean/ComparatorChallenges/OstmannComplete.lean` (statements `inverseGoldbach` and `twoInfiniteSummandsImpossible`) and `lean/ComparatorChallenges/OstmannPrimes.lean` (statement `main`, the symmetric difference of $A+B$A+B with the primes is infinite for nontrivial $A,B\\subseteq\\mathbb N$A,B⊆N); both comparator files state their theorems with `sorry` in place of a proof.\n\nErdősLibraryPrimesOpenai 2026 additive indecomposability primes\n\nUpdated Oct 8\n\n# Openai 2026 additive indecomposability primes\n\n../\n\ntheorem_1_1: Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture as the manuscript claims it