2026\n\nLean notes ↗\n\n## All quoted lines (3)\n\n  1. 1\n\n> Our proof uses the simultaneous residue restrictions and coverage of every sufficiently large prime to reach a contradiction without such a classification.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nErdős #431: Ostmann's inverse Goldbach problem — OpenAI's 80-page proof that the primes are not a sumset holds as read by four AI referee seats; its Lean is two proofs, and the second runs through a formal proof that Landau–Siegel zeros do not exist, which the kernel accepts against our own statement of it — Erdős Problem a Day (https://www.erdosproblemaday.com/day/431-openai-ostmann-inverse-goldbach-refereed)\nciteturn6search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: yesterday; That is a short sieve proof of Laffer and Mann's reduction (Lemma 2.2). ... It opened Laffer and Mann's paper: Theorem 12 says what the paper says, though their proof goes through Dirichlet's theorem rather than a sieve.\n  * the symmetric difference is infinite: either A + B misses infinitely many primes, or it contains infinitely many composites (or both);\n  * so if A + B contains every large prime, it contains infinitely many composite numbers. The paper says this, and the Lean proves it as `infinitely_many_composite_sums` (in route M's `Corollaries.lean`).\n\nIt is not true that both always happen. Take A = {1, 3} and B = {2, 4}: A + B = {3, 5, 7} contains no composite at all, and misses every prime but three. Thomas Bloom's 8 October summary of the release states the theorem with \"and\" (\"infinitely many composite numbers in A+B, and infinitely many primes not in A+B\"), which is stronger than the theorem and false; read it as \"or\".\n\nElsholtz and Harper work with positive integers and the paper with ℕ₀, which includes 0. Sets of positive integers are the special case 0 ∉ A ∪ B, so the theorem covers both readings.\n\nThe argument. The proof is by contradiction from a decomposition, and it uses both halves of the hypothesis separately: eventual exclusion of composites from A + B, and eventual coverage of the primes by it.\n\n  * §2 first disposes of finite summands. If A were finite, two of its elements a₁ ≠ a₂ would make b + a₁ and b + a₂ both prime for every large b ∈ B, and a sieve for finitely many shifts (Lemma 2.1) gives B(Y) ≪ Y/(log Y)², too few to cover the ≍ Y/log Y primes up to Y with only |A| shifts. That is a short sieve proof of Laffer and Mann's reduction (Lemma 2.2). What is left is Theorem 2.3, two infinite summands. Put D = −B. Exclusion does the first work: if a ∈ A and b ∈ B are large and a ≡ −b (mod p), then p divides a + b > p, so a + b is composite. So for each prime p the large elements of A and of D have disjoint images in 𝔽_{p}. Completing them to a partition gives a residue set S_{p} and its complement, and a collision estimate shows that on suitable averages over primes the two parts have about half of 𝔽_{p} each, with A and D close to uniform on them.\n  * §3 rules out persistent correlation of the partition with quadratic characters whose translating centre may be chosen independently at each prime. \n  * R1 checked the reduction to infinite summands in full, including a summand that contains 0, a finite summand on either side, and small primes; exclusion supplies the two shifts and coverage supplies π(Y) ≤ |A|·B(Y) + O(1). It opened Laffer and Mann's paper: Theorem 12 says what the paper says, though their proof goes through Dirichlet's theorem rather than a sieve. It opened Elsholtz 2006: Theorem 1.9 has exactly the paper's exponents, √N(log N)^{−3} ≪ A(N) ≪ √N(log N)², for positive integers, and the paper reproves it for ℕ₀ as Lemma 2.4. Elsholtz–Harper's sharper bounds are quoted correctly in §1 and used nowhere after.\n  * R1 rebuilt §3 step by step: the amplified moment, the Poisson and Gauss-sum identity behind (3.9) (checked numerically to 6·10^{−12}; with the sign of h₀ flipped the error is 91.6), the moment bound over a family fixed before sampling, the common rational centre, Heath-Brown's quadratic large sieve as used at (3.28) (compared with the Lean's transcription, because the 1995 paper could not be retrieved), the exceptional-zero step and the final collision count. R3 checked that every possible exceptional modulus is handled, not only the generic one: a conductor with a large prime factor is kept out by deleting that prime from every nongiant prior, and a conductor made of small primes can only touch the frequency part. (§3 does use Siegel's theorem, in its ineffective form, which is fine for a qualitative statement.)\n  * L re-derived every display of §9 and checked each earlier result it invokes at the point of use, including the order in which the constants are fixed: B_{s}, then B_{*}, B_{z}, B_{D}, the depth k, and finally L → ∞. Its brute-force count of bad arrangements is tight at r = 4, m = 2 (384 against the 384 allowed by the counting step in the proof of (9.1)).\n  * R2 reconstructed §4, the higher-order characters, including the transfer, the history uniqueness and the diagonal. The anchor-code bookkeeping of (4.9)–(4.13) was simulated exactly for depths 1–6: every code occurs on at most two paths, and two paths with different codes always have an anchor with net exponents (±2, 0) or (0, ±2). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOstmann’s inverse Goldbach theorem and the infinite-summands case (Lean) | Plectis Atlas (https://wcook04.github.io/plectis-atlas/statements/OstmannComplete.html)\nciteturn6search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Shared only with the release’s name for the Lean statement or OpenAI’s note on it: ostmanns. ...     `import Mathlib namespace OAI open scoped Pointwise symmDiff namespace Ostmann def primes : Set ℕ := {n | Nat.Prime n}abbrev sumset (A B : Set ℕ) : Set ℕ := A + B def EventuallyPrimeSumset (A B : Set ℕ) : Prop :=  ∃ N : ℕ, ∀ n, N ≤ n → (n ∈ sumset A B ↔ n.